  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 10,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.088,
            0.89,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "used to indicate the number of atoms of each element in the molecule. The first element retains its regular name, while the second element ends in -ide. Prefixes, such as mono- (1), di- (2), tri- (3), tetra- (4), penta- (5), hexa- (6), etc., indicate the number of atoms. However, mono- is usually omitted for the first element but is used for the second element. If a prefix ends with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ \\circ 0 ^ { \\prime }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\bf \\Phi } ^ { \\circ } { \\bf a } ^ { \\prime }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and the element starts with a vowel, drop the last vowel (for example, monoxide, pentoxide). If the prefix ends with "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\bar { \\mathrm { i } } \\bar { \\mathrm { j } } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " keep it for pronunciation (for example, dioxide, trioxide)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.228,
            0.325,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Examples"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.231,
            0.25,
            0.712,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y CO is named as carbon monoxide (not monooxide)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.267,
            0.779,
            0.285
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is named as carbon dioxide (not monocarbon dioxide)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.286,
            0.79,
            0.303
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbb { C } \\mathbb { S } _ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is named as carbon disulfide, showing two sulfur atoms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.304,
            0.887,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { P C l } _ { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is named as phosphorus trichloride, showing three chlorine atoms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.322,
            0.827,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { S F _ { 6 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is named as sulfur hexafluoride, showing six fluorine atoms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.34,
            0.739,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { N _ { 2 } O _ { 4 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is named as dinitrogen tetroxide (not tetraoxide)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.358,
            0.606,
            0.376
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { N } _ { 2 } \\mathrm { O } _ { 5 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is named as dinitrogen pentoxide."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.378,
            0.888,
            0.429
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When hydrogen is the first element in the formula, no prefix is added before hydrogen, irrespective of the number of its atoms. For example, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { H } _ { 2 } \\mathrm { S }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is named hydrogen sulfide, not dihydrogen sulfide."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.428,
            0.889,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A few binary compounds are known only by their common names. For example, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { H _ { 2 } O _ { \\circ } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " which would usually be named hydrogen monoxide, is commonly known as water. Similarly, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{NH_{3}}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " which is actually nitrogen trihydride, is known as ammonia."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.505,
            0.755,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.4.2 Bonding by electron transfer — Ionic bond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.527,
            0.89,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If the valence shell of an atom has less than four electrons, it would generally donate its valence electrons to achieve a stable electronic configuration. Identify four such elements among the first 18 elements by using their electronic configurations given in Table 8.4 of the Chapter 8, Journey Inside the Atom. Atoms with more than 4 valence electrons usually gain or share electrons to complete an octet."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.628,
            0.888,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You are familiar with sodium chloride (common salt), whose chemical formula is NaCl. Let us take the bonding in NaCl as an example."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.662,
            0.889,
            0.73
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The atomic number of sodium is 11. Its valence shell contains only one electron, which can attain a stable electronic configuration after losing this valence electron. Will it still be neutral after losing one electron? If not, what charge would it carry and why?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.729,
            0.888,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When sodium atom (Fig. 9.11a) loses its valence electron, it becomes a positively charged species, called a sodium "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "cation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", represented as Na (Fig. 9.11b). It is so because it would have 11 protons and 10 electrons."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.295,
            0.783,
            0.828,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": [
